<template>
  <div class="field" :class="{ dark: dark }">
    <input
      type="text"
      class="input"
      :value="value"
      :placeholder="placeholder"
      @input="on_input"
    />
  </div>
</template>

<script setup>
// The composer field. It owns how the field LOOKS and nothing else: the text
// belongs to `Todos`, and so does the decision of what happens when the user
// types.
//
// `.field` exists only to hold the theme class, and it cannot be styled by a
// `.dark …` rule in this same block: the cascade matches a selector's leftmost
// part only against a STRICT ancestor (velox-style/src/lib.rs `match_prefix`),
// and the element carrying the class is never its own ancestor. So the carrier
// is a wrapper and the surface lives one level down.
//
// The field's own defaults are declared here rather than inherited from the
// page, so the component is usable on its own. `dark` is a prop because of the
// wrapper note in the template above.
//
// The author's `padding`, `background` and `border` on an `<input>` are honoured
// by the renderer, and `::placeholder` is a real pseudo-class: the cascade keeps
// those declarations on a SEPARATE `style:placeholder` attribute
// (velox-style/src/lib.rs:786, `PLACEHOLDER_STYLE_ATTR`) so they cannot repaint
// the value, and the box painter reads them when the value is empty
// (velox-renderer/src/skia_render.rs `placeholder_color`). A placeholder's
// colour is otherwise DERIVED from the field's own text colour, so the one below
// is a decision rather than an absence.
pub struct Props {
    pub value: String,
    pub placeholder: String,
    pub dark: bool,
}

pub struct State {
    pub props: Props,
}

impl State {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            props: Props {
                value: String::new(),
                placeholder: String::from("What needs to be done?"),
                dark: false,
            },
        }
    }

    pub fn value(&self) -> String {
        self.props.value.clone()
    }

    pub fn placeholder(&self) -> String {
        self.props.placeholder.clone()
    }

    pub fn dark(&self) -> bool {
        self.props.dark
    }

    // The root dispatcher routes a typed event to the owning `Todos` state, so
    // this signature exists only to keep the generated component handler well
    // formed. `Todos::on_input` is where the text actually lands.
    pub fn on_input(&self) {}
}
</script>

<style scoped>
/* The field is the composer's INSET, not a box inside it: it has no edge and no
   fill of its own, and its inset is its own `padding`. The one edge on this
   control is the bar's, in Todos.vx — two nested rounded boxes is what made the
   old page read as a form. The scales are declared once, at the top of App.vx. */
.field {
    display: flex;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
    font-family: "Inter", "Noto Sans", "DejaVu Sans", sans-serif;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.45;
}

/* 40px tall, with the text 12px from the plane's inner edge: the two numbers
   that decide whether a text field feels like a control or like a line in a
   paragraph. It matches `.add`, so the bar is exactly as tall as the two
   controls inside it. The `#ffffff` is not decoration — the renderer paints a
   WHITE fallback fill on any `<input>` that declares no background
   (velox-style/src/ua.css), so saying it out loud is the only way to reach the
   card fill the bar already has. `min-width: 0` is what lets the field shrink
   inside the composer's flex row instead of refusing to.

   `border: 1px solid transparent` is how "no edge of its own" is spelled here,
   and it is load-bearing rather than a no-op. An `<input>` that declares NO
   author border is not left unpainted: the box painter falls back to a hard 1px
   `#c8c8c8` outline centred on the field's own edge
   (velox-renderer/src/skia_render.rs, `field_radius` fallback). Against the
   composer's plane that grey line read as a SECOND, square-cornered box inside
   the bar — a bright vertical edge a few pixels left of `.add`, which is the
   "the Add button is overlapping the input box" report. `border: 0` is not the
   way out: the same painter forces `stroke_width = 1.0` whenever an author
   border exists at all, so `0` still draws a 1px line. An author border of a
   zero-alpha colour is what actually suppresses it, and `transparent` is
   understood by both the cascade (velox-dom/src/style.rs) and the painter.

   The 1px is a real border box, so the side padding is 11px to keep the text
   12px from the plane's inner edge — exactly where it was before this border
   existed — and `box-sizing: border-box` (velox-style/src/ua.css `*`) keeps the
   field 40px tall. */
.input {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 40px;
    padding: 0 11px;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    background: #ffffff;
    color: #14181b;
    font-size: 15px;
}
/* The muted ink role — 4.75:1 on the inset fill, where the `#667076` this
   replaces was 4.33:1 and failed 4.5 outright. */
.input::placeholder {
    color: #5F6A70;
}

/* Dark overrides — source order decides the winner, so they come last. */
.dark .input {
    background: #141A1E;
    color: #EDF2F3;
}
.dark .input::placeholder {
    color: #849299;
}
</style>
